```matlab
% Define parameters
population_size = 100;
mutation_rate = 0.01;
selection_pressure = 0.5;

% Initialize population
bacteria = zeros(population_size, 1);
bacteria(1:population_size) = 'A';

% Simulate evolution over time
for i = 1:100
    % Reproduce bacteria
    new_population = bacteria * population_size;

    % Mutate some bacteria
    mutated_population = new_population .* (1 - mutation_rate);
    mutated_population(randi([1 population_size], mutation_rate * population_size)) = 'B';

    % Select bacteria based on fitness
    fitness_A = ones(population_size, 1);
    fitness_B = fitness_A .* (1 + selection_pressure);
    selected_population = mutated_population(randperm(population_size * 2, population_size) ./ (fitness_A + fitness_B));

    % Update population
    bacteria = selected_population;
end

% Print final population
disp(['Final population:']);
disp(bacteria);
```

In this code, we start with a population of only type A bacteria, and then simulate the evolution of the population over 100 generations. We introduce mutations that can change the type of bacteria from A to B, and we select bacteria based on their fitness, which is determined by their resistance to the antibiotic. As the simulation progresses, we see the frequency of type B bacteria increase, indicating that natural selection is favoring the more resistant type.

